@@ -0,0 +1,31 @@
+# Available editor viewport
+
+An annotation surface may implement `setViewport(rect, refresh)`. Ink Away
+reports the screen space remaining after its toolbar, before intersecting it
+with the surface's content clip. The rectangle follows toolbar side, thickness
+and visibility. A provider may refit its content there or retain overlay layout.
+No toolbar-position logic or provider identifiers are required in the provider.
+
+Return true when the provider has rebuilt widgets whose screen bounds need a
+snapshot before their clip can be read. The editor skips the stale clip, paints
+the new background, then reads the clip and loads saved ink in the new geometry.
+This also happens when navigation hands the editor a new surface. Repeated
+reports of the same rectangle need no extra provider reflow.
+
+The editor anchors pending edits before toolbar layout changes and reloads its
+editable operations after a provider reflow. A provider can call
+`refresh(reflow)` for its own preference changes: the editor saves against the
+old geometry, calls `reflow()`, then snapshots/reloads. Like loading a different
+page, reloading replaces the canvas's local editing history. Saved annotation
+coordinates and untouched item identities are preserved. Discard the callback
+and restore the provider's ordinary layout in `finish()`.
+
+Providers without this optional method retain the existing overlay behavior.
+Document-surface version 1 remains backward compatible.
+
+`luajit tests/surface_viewport.lua` uses the actual book, canvas and toolbar
+lifecycle against an unrelated generic provider. It covers initial layout,
+all toolbar sides, collapse/reopen, a provider preference change, snapshot-before-
+clip ordering, newly drawn/native ink preservation and cleanup. Host widgets,
+rendering and persistence are fixtures. Source-viewer integration tests separately
+verify the provider's real layout and animation behavior.
